" cy="16.5" r="1"/></svg></span><span><strong>About $1,200</strong><span class="t">tax a year per $100,000 on a rental</span></span></li><li><span class="i"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M15 9.4c-.4-1-1.5-1.8-3-1.8-1.8 0-3 1-3 2.2 0 3 6 1.6 6 4.4 0 1.3-1.3 2.2-3 2.2-1.6 0-2.7-.7-3.1-1.8M12 5.8v1.8M12 16.4v1.8"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/></svg></span><span><strong>23 to 48%</strong><span class="t">of a 2-bedroom condo's rent goes to dues</span></span></li></ul></div><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem">By <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Devin Day</strong>, Operations Officer &middot; Reviewed by <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Tim Nash</strong>, Broker-in-Charge &middot; <span style="white-space:nowrap">Updated November 2, 2026</span></p><p class="detail-sub">For a year-round rental in Horry County. It uses the tax a rental pays in each town, HUD rents by ZIP code, condo dues from homes for sale, and insurance.</p></div></div><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The short answer</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Usually not, for cash flow. Take out the tax, dues, management and repairs. The middle house for sale in Carolina Forest or Longs then keeps about 4 to 5 percent of its price a year, before insurance and any loan. A condo keeps about 1.4 percent. Our target is 6. The rest is the rise in price, which beat our 3 percent target over five and ten years.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The tax is higher than the listing shows. A rental pays about $1,200 a year for each $100,000 of value outside city limits, about 3 times what an owner who lives there pays. The year after you buy, the county resets the value to about what you paid.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="calculator"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Calculator">How do you run the numbers on a Myrtle Beach rental?</h2><style>
